% ============================================================================
% valve-alternate-name — the everyday ALTERNATE name a source gives for a
% heart valve, decoded from a clause already sitting unused inside
% `heart-valves.adj`'s and `valve-kind.adj`'s own already-quoted NCI SEER
% source sentence (adj-facts-stdlib, ANATOMY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `heart-valves.adj`
% (`valve_separates(valve, boundary)`, mitral → left_atrium_and_left_ventricle)
% and `valve-kind.adj` (`valve_kind(valve, kind)`, mitral → atrioventricular):
% both of those tables' own already-quoted NCI SEER span ALSO names an
% everyday alternate name for the mitral valve -- a fact neither the boundary
% nor the kind schema had room for:
%
%     "The left atrioventricular valve is the bicuspid, or mitral, valve."
%
% New `valve_alternate_name(valve, alt_name)` table decodes that span as its
% own row: mitral → bicuspid. "Alt_name" (an everyday synonym for the valve)
% is categorically distinct from "boundary" (the two chambers/vessels it
% separates) and "kind" (its physiological classification) -- the mitral
% valve's boundary is left_atrium_and_left_ventricle and its kind is
% atrioventricular, but its alternate name is a separate fact neither column
% has room for. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? valve_alternate_name(mitral, $AltName)   % bicuspid
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like its
% siblings' own): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `valve_alternate_name(valve, alt_name)` carrying the citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query -- the engine returns the
% alternate name WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on anything that is not the mitral valve (it never invents an
% alternate name). The query also runs BACKWARD -- bind an alternate name
% and recall the valve it names:
%
%     ? valve_alternate_name($Valve, bicuspid)   % mitral
%
% A recall on `tricuspid` -- a real, already-tabled valve in
% `heart-valves.adj`, but whose own quoted span ("The right atrioventricular
% valve is the tricuspid valve.") never supplies a second, everyday name for
% the valve itself -- correctly abstains rather than inventing one for it.
% Likewise `pulmonary` and `aortic` are deliberately left unrowed: their own
% quoted spans never supply an alternate name either.
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME sentence already quoted
% inside both `heart-valves.adj`'s and `valve-kind.adj`'s own headers, from
% the SAME NCI SEER Training Modules "Structure of the Heart" page those
% tables already cite -- no new WebFetch, only a different schema decoding a
% different clause of that already-verified quote. `trust authoritative` --
% the same tier the sibling tables already carry (NCI SEER Training is a
% primary U.S. government reference).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts, feedback_no_byte_arithmetic_for_llm.)
% ============================================================================

table valve_alternate_name {
    columns valve, alt_name

    row (mitral, bicuspid)

    source "The left atrioventricular valve is the bicuspid, or mitral, valve."
    locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/cardiovascular/heart/structure.html"
    trust authoritative
}
